trace_id": "step_05", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_06", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_08", "role": "support"}], "narrative": "Neighbourhood-turnover series for the group (half-window 5000) sits at a low baseline of 0.06\u20130.17 through t\u224813700, then rises steeply (0.42 at 14217, 0.64 at 15756, 0.97 at 16268) and saturates at peak_change = 1.00 at t=16781, decaying afterwards \u2014 a single sharp transition, not spread-out drift. Peak dissimilarity 1.0 >> 0.6 threshold. The before/after contact sets are completely disjoint (step_01: {123,208,283,497,616,829,899,1064,...} -> {160,350,462,861,871,945,1057,1088,...}); diff_snapshots on the group's edges over the two 5000-unit halves gives Jaccard 0.0 with 135 edges removed and 96 added (step_03). Event counts confirm a switch, not a fade: contacts with the old partner set collapse from 308/159/53/36 events to 1 event in [16501,17500], while the new partner set rises from ~6-9 to 138 and 624 events (steps 10-11). Confounder controls: the group's own activity volume is roughly flat across the period (112, 88, 100, 97, 97, 50, 46, 106, 86, 89, 116, 106 per bin, step_02) and per-node degrees remain comparable before and after (steps 04-05), so the change is in partner identity rather than volume or a popularity surge; the graph-wide diff over the same two halves (step_06) shows global churn is generic, but the group's own baseline turnover was near zero before 14000, isolating the abrupt shift; and the displaced old partners show the mirror-image switch away from the group (step_08)."}, "raw_final_response": "```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_c1b49572\",\n  \"decision\": \"present\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [214, 315, 522, 860, 919, 1085, 1390, 1431, 1589, 1672, 1860, 1936],\n  \"window\": [2551, 32551],\n  \"confidence\": 0.92,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"support\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"Neighbourhood-turnover series for the group (half-window 5000) sits at a low baseline of 0.06\u20130.17 through t\u224813700, then rises steeply (0.42 at 14217, 0.64 at 15756, 0.97 at 16268) and saturates at peak_change = 1.00 at t=16781, decaying afterwards \u2014 a single sharp transition, not spread-out drift. Peak dissimilarity 1.0 >> 0.6 threshold. The before/after contact sets are completely disjoint (step_01: {123,208,283,497,616,829,899,1064,...} -> {160,350,462,861,871,945,1057,1088,...}); diff_snapshots on the group's edges over the two 5000-unit halves gives Jaccard 0.0 with 135 edges removed and 96 added (step_03). Event counts confirm a switch, not a fade: contacts with the old partner set collapse from 308/159/53/36 events to 1 event in [16501,17500], while the new partner set rises from ~6-9 to 138 and 624 events (steps 10-11). Confounder controls: the group's own activity volume is roughly flat across the period (112, 88, 100, 97, 97, 50, 46, 106, 86, 89, 116, 106 per bin, step_02) and per-node degrees remain comparable before and after (steps 04-05), so the change is in partner identity rather than volume or a popularity surge; the graph-wide diff over the same two halves (step_06) shows global churn is generic, but the group's own baseline turnover was near zero before 14000, isolating the abrupt shift; and the displaced old partners show the mirror-image switch away from the group (step_08).\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 49134, "total_output_tokens": 3782, "total_tokens": 5